think Confirmed the gap by measurement, not memory: grepped every image/font5x7_*.rye for uppercase Latin-1 codepoints. Missing capitals are U+00CB, U+00CF, U+00D0, U+00D5, U+00DD, U+00DE. The Icelandic three (D0 Eth, DD Y acute, DE Thorn) are the crux-first, set-closing rung mirroring the lowercase corpus; the other three (CB, CF, D5 diaeresis/tilde capitals) are a distinct gap I named honestly rather than folded in -- one keystone per round.
think Modeled image/font5x7_upper_icelandic.rye on font5x7_upper_nordic.rye and font5x7_icelandic.rye: the three-distinct-constructions crux, drawings-are-the-spec, explicit ascending codepoints, gfa1 round-trip byte-identical, per-construction toothed guards. Eth is a full-height capital D crossed by a bar (top+bottom bar closing the bowl, a crossbar on the middle row with left-half ink == 2 crossing the stem into the bowl); Thorn is a full-height stem open at top and bottom (stem col inked and full-row ink == 1 on rows 0 and 6) with a bowl at its middle; Y acute is the compressed capital Y with the acute leaning right on rows 0..1 (top-row right-half inked, left-half blank), the same lean the upper_accented corpus proves.
obs Y acute is inherently sparse (ink 9) while Eth is heavy (ink 17), so the honest legibility band widened to 9..20 -- the floor still catches a blank glyph, the band names the true spread rather than a false-tight one. Compression stays a reported fact, never asserted; the round-trip is the proof.
